This repository is Dinamic Attendance, a production attendance and workforce-management system with WhatsApp workflows, operations, work groups, schedules, absences, notifications, administrative actions, reporting, and SQL Server persistence.

Apply the global engineering rules plus the following project-specific constraints.

Domain integrity:
- Preserve company, user, employee, operation, work-group, attendance, absence, schedule, and notification boundaries.
- Do not assume that one employee has only one eligible shift, operation, or work period per day.
- Changes involving shifts, slots, schedules, or work dates must consider multiple valid work periods on the same date.
- Preserve compatibility with operations that use a single schedule as well as operations that use multiple shifts.
- Do not collapse distinct attendance journeys into one record unless the domain explicitly requires it.

Attendance rules:
- Treat server time as authoritative where the existing system does so.
- Preserve configured tolerances, grace periods, early/late rules, and company-specific settings.
- Do not duplicate attendance-rule calculations across WhatsApp, admin UI, operations, reports, or background jobs when an existing shared rule can be reused.
- Manual attendance and WhatsApp attendance must preserve their distinct source/audit semantics.
- Manual administrative actions must not fabricate coordinates or WhatsApp evidence.
- Preserve valid cases such as missing arrival, missing departure, approved absence, late arrival, early departure, and manually validated attendance.

Authorization:
- Enforce permissions on the backend even when UI controls are hidden.
- Users must not gain privileges through self-edit flows.
- Preserve protections against users disabling themselves or modifying equal/higher privileged users where applicable.
- Administrative attendance actions must remain restricted to authorized internal roles.

WhatsApp and notifications:
- Treat WhatsApp messages as externally observable side effects.
- Avoid duplicate sends and preserve idempotency.
- Be careful with retries, scheduled jobs, leases, notification thresholds, and delivery state.
- Do not increase WhatsApp volume unless explicitly required.
- Preserve the distinction between urgent WhatsApp alerts and scheduled email/reporting workflows.

Persistence and concurrency:
- Follow existing SQL Server transaction and migration conventions.
- Attendance creation/replacement/update flows must be safe under concurrent processing.
- Background jobs must preserve existing lease/idempotency semantics.
- Migrations must remain compatible with existing attendance history.

Testing:
- Prioritize boundary-time cases, multiple shifts, timezone/server-time behavior, permissions, duplicate processing, retries, manual attendance, absences, and concurrent updates.
- Do not claim WhatsApp or email delivery succeeded unless the corresponding integration or execution evidence confirms it.

